<clickhouse>
    <!--
      PRODUCTION ClickHouse profile — for a DEDICATED box (>= ~32GB RAM, >= 8 vCPU),
      NOT co-located with Postgres/gateway/portals.

      Counterpart to tuning.xml (the "small" survival profile for the 1.4GB
      co-located test container). Select this one by setting, in .env.prod:

          CH_TUNING_FILE=tuning.prod.xml
          CH_MEM_LIMIT=48g          # or unset the container mem_limit entirely
          CH_CPUS=0                 # 0 = no cpu cap on a dedicated box

      Then recreate ONLY clickhouse:
          docker compose ... up -d --force-recreate clickhouse

      Key difference from the small profile: this KEEPS ClickHouse's real
      defaults. Self-telemetry stays ON (observability), caches and memory scale
      to the box. The small profile disabled all of that purely to survive
      1.4GB — carrying those settings onto a big box would cripple it.
    -->

    <!--
      Memory: use the box's RAM via the default ratio, stated explicitly.
      0 = "no fixed cap, use max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio". Do NOT set a
      tiny fixed ceiling here (the small profile pinned it to 1GB).
    -->
    <max_server_memory_usage>0</max_server_memory_usage>
    <max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio>0.9</max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio>

    <!--
      Caches: intentionally NOT overridden — ClickHouse's defaults (mark ~5GB,
      uncompressed ~8GB, index-mark ~5GB) are correct for a dedicated box and are
      what make queries fast on real data volume. The small profile shrank these
      to 256MB/0/128MB, which would thrash here.
    -->

    <!--
      Self-telemetry tables (system.metric_log, trace_log, asynchronous_metric_log,
      processors_profile_log, query_log, part_log, …) are ENABLED by default and
      intentionally NOT removed here. You want them in production for metrics
      history and query-trace debugging. The small profile removed them because
      their merges could not fit in 1.4GB and spiraled — a problem that only
      exists on a starved box.

      Keep their disk bounded with a TTL rather than by disabling them. Example
      (30-day retention on the metric log):
    -->
    <metric_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 30 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </metric_log>

    <!--
      Thread pools: left at defaults so ClickHouse uses the box's cores. The
      small profile throttled these (schedule 16 / max 256 / concurrent 16) for
      2 cores; do not carry that here.
    -->

    <!--
      NOTE: SYS_NICE is a container capability (cap_add in
      docker-compose.clickhouse.yml), not an XML setting — it stays for ALL box
      sizes. Without it ClickHouse spams "get_mempolicy: Operation not permitted".
    -->
</clickhouse>
